    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, our offensive engine is centered on **Corbin Carroll** (85.94) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (72.72), both of whom benefit immensely from a favorable matchup against Tomoyuki Sugano (5.02 xERA). **Corbin Carroll** is our premier play; his elite sprint speed remains our primary weapon in this 5x5 format, and facing a high-xERA arm in a favorable park environment gives him the highest ceiling of the day. **Brice Turang** (68.98) also earns a high-confidence start; despite the name value of Walker Buehler, the underlying metrics suggest **Brice Turang** is in a prime position to capitalize on his leadoff spot and stolen base potential. We are also monitoring **Gabriel Moreno** (71.17) closely; if he lands in the #3 hole as projected, his xwOBA profile against Sugano makes him a vital anchor for our daily production.\n\nThe most difficult decision was the final outfield slot, where **Mickey Moniak** (60.31) narrowly edged out the lower-scoring options. While **Mickey Moniak** faces a struggling Mitch Bratt (5.81 xERA), his score is tempered by a significant platoon disadvantage. Conversely, **Zach McKinstry** (42.59) falls into the marginal bucket today due to a poor BvP history and the presence of a catcher with a quick pop-time (1.86s), which severely suppresses his stolen base viability. We are playing a cautious game with **Travis Bazzana** (41.97) and **Cole Young** (41.01), both of whom are hovering just above our \"below floor\" threshold; they remain in the lineup solely for volume, as their matchups against tough arms like Anderson and Weathers offer little margin for error. \n\nRisk management remains paramount today, particularly with the \"Lineup Pending\" designations for **Gabriel Moreno**, **Isaac Paredes**, **Max Muncy**, **Tyler Soderstrom**, **Mickey Moniak**, **Corbin Carroll**, and **Brice Turang**. Should these statuses shift, we risk losing the projected batting order advantages that buoyed their scores. We are 